-- title:
--   Bad geometric fibres of the two-chart model of X₁(Mp)
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and $M$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$; a field $L$ of characteristic zero that is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ for $\{p\}$, with a primitive $p$-th root of unity $\zeta \in L$; and an intermediate field $K$ of $L$ in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ equal to the $L$-subfield generated by the image of the function field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L137) under the coefficientwise map [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81). Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ and $\zeta$ is in the image of $A \to L$, with an $A$-algebra structure on $K$ compatible with $A \to L \to K$, and let $j \in K$, $j \neq 0$, have Laurent expansion [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81). Write $f =$ [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) for the structure morphism of the two-chart pushout model $X =$ [`ModularCurve.TwoChartModel A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L229) to $\operatorname{Spec} A$. Let $U \subseteq X$ be an open subscheme with $U \hookrightarrow X$ followed by $f$ smooth of relative dimension $1$, maximal among opens with that property, and let $\varepsilon$ be a section of $f$ (a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} A \to X$ composing with $f$ to the identity) whose image lies in $U$. The conclusion asserts: for every algebraically closed field $k$ and every $s : \operatorname{Spec} k \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ for which the geometric fibre $X_s \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ (the second pullback projection of $f$ along $s$) is not smooth, there are schemes $C_1, C_2$ with morphisms $c_\nu : C_\nu \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ that are proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and satisfy `GeometricallyIntegral`, closed immersions $i_\nu : C_\nu \to X_s$ over $k$ (so $i_\nu$ followed by $X_s \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ is $c_\nu$), and an $n \in \mathbb{N}$ such that: every point of $X_s$ lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; the fibre product $C_1 \times_{X_s} C_2$ is reduced with exactly $n$ points, and $n > 0$; the point $e \in X_s$ obtained from $\varepsilon$ and $s$ as the image of the closed point of $\operatorname{Spec} k$ under `sectionFibrePoint ε s` lies in the image of $i_1$ but not of $i_2$; the preimage $U_s$ of $U$ in $X_s$ is the complement of the image of $C_1 \times_{X_s} C_2 \to C_1 \to X_s$; the image of $i_1$ meets $U_s$ exactly in the connected component of $e$ in $U_s$, while the image of $i_2$ meets $U_s$ in the complement in $U_s$ of that component; and there are opens $W_1, W_2$ of $X_s$ whose underlying sets are the complements of the images of $i_2$, resp. $i_1$, such that the inclusion of $i_\nu^{-1} W_\nu$ followed by $i_\nu$ is an open immersion.
--
--   This is the formal counterpart of the classical description of the degenerate (characteristic $p$) geometric fibres of a model of $X_1(Mp)$ as two proper smooth geometrically integral curves crossing at finitely many points — the Igusa components meeting at the supersingular points — with the maximal smooth open $U$ cutting out the complement of the crossings and the section $\varepsilon$ landing on the first component. It is phrased exactly as the degeneracy hypothesis required by the relative-Picard representability machinery, and is used by the results on representability of the relative sub-Picard scheme, the modular-unit dictionary, and the behaviour of base-changed sections under component swapping.
